## III. INSTITUTIONAL FEEDBACK LOOPS AND PRINCIPAL-AGENT DRIFT

When an adverse-selection importation pipeline operates continuously over multiple decades, it alters the equilibrium behavior of the host system’s domestic institutions in two predictable ways.

First, **verification standards erode endogenously.** When university administrators, compliance officers, and corporate boards observe that merit and security criteria are routinely waived in exchange for endowment contributions or sovereign capital access, the signaling value of the credential decays across the entire system (Goodhart’s Law). Domestic actors adjust their behavior to match the observed incentive structure, shifting effort away from technical and operational rigor toward relationship-building and rent-seeking.

Second, **high-friction administrative hierarchies ($C_s > 0$) favor political arbitrage over technical competence.** Within buffered corporate and regulatory organizations where performance cannot be immediately falsified by market competition, operators selected from patronage-oriented environments hold a comparative advantage in coalition-building and backchannel coordination relative to field-independent engineers ($G_f$). Over time, this dynamic displaces technical domain specialists from critical Command-and-Control (C2) decision nodes, reducing the organization’s requisite variety ($\mathcal{V}_{\text{Control}} < \mathcal{V}_{\text{Environment}}$) when confronted with Out-of-Distribution (OOD) operational shocks (`AUDIT-2026-CYBERNETIC-HUMAN-ROUTER-v3.0`).

